// The `array` sub-library backs the native dynamic and fixed-size array types
// (`T[]`, `T[N]`). It has no callable API: methods such as `push`, `pop` and
// `length` are exposed by the language itself.
//
// Under the hood it generates `core_array.h`, which implements a growable
// `Buffer` reused across the whole runtime. Several techniques keep heap
// fragmentation low on these memory-constrained targets:
//
// - Single contiguous block: an `Array<T>` is one `realloc`-managed region and
//   elements are placement-constructed in place, so there is never one
//   allocation per element.
// - Geometric growth: capacity grows as `capacity + capacity/divisor + 1`
//   (~1.25x by default, divisor configurable per platform). Repeated `push`es
//   only reallocate every few appends, amortizing cost and reducing heap churn.
// - Minimum capacity floor (4) and a u16 saturation guard so capacity never
//   accidentally shrinks on overflow.
// - `pop` shrinks by halves, and only when `size <= capacity/2` with capacity
//   above the minimum, releasing memory in coarse blocks instead of one byte
//   at a time.
// - Integer-only growth math avoids pulling in the float library.
// - `reserve` sizes a single allocation up front (initializer lists and
//   copies), and move assignment hands off the buffer pointer (`stealFrom`)
//   with no reallocation.
// - Fixed-size arrays (`T[N]`) live on the stack as a compile-time buffer, so
//   they allocate no heap at all and cannot fragment it.
